      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 2 of 2 of a certificate fragment. It contains:\n\n1. Page 5 of 6: Table 3 - General characteristics of the indicating device, plus production locations\n2. Page 6 of 6: Certificate history (revision history)\n\nCertificate info:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02, revision 3\n- Project number: 3846781\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Page total: 6 (pages 5 and 6 shown)\n- Issuing authority: NMi (Netherlands Measurinstituut, presumably)\n\nTable 3: General characteristics of the indicating device\n- Columns: Meter size, Indicating range (minimum value) [m³], Verification scale interval (maximum value) [m³]\n- Rows:\n  - DN25, DN40 and DN50: 99.999.999, 0,0001\n  - DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125 and DN150: 99.999.999, 0,001\n  - DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400 and DN450: 99.999.999, 0,01\n  - DN500 and DN600: 99.999.999, 0,1\n\nNote: \"99.999.999\" — this is likely 99,999,999 (99 million) using European thousands separators with dots. Actually in the context of water meters, indicating range minimum value is typically 99999.999 m³. Hmm. Let me think.\n\nWater meter registers typically have 8 digits: 99999.999 m³ display. The \"indicating range (minimum value)\" of 99.999.999 with dots as thousand separators would be 99999999 m³? That seems too large. Actually for a DN600 water meter, max flow is very high. Actually, let me reconsider: In R49 water meters, the indicating device register typically displays 99999.999 m³. The \"indicating range\" minimum value would be the full-scale reading. The value \"99.999.999\" is ambiguous — with dots as thousands separators it'd be 99,999,999 which is about 100 million m³. That's unusual.\n\nActually, it could be \"99 999.999 m³\" which is 99999.999 m³ (~100,000 m³). That's a typical 8-digit register with 3 decimals. The dots are thousands separators: 99.999.999 = 99,999,999? Hmm, but with three decimals it would be 99 999.999. The OCR shows \"99.999.999\" which has two separators. If the last dot is a decimal point, it's 99 999.999 (with first dot as thousands separator) = 99999.999. That makes sense: 99999.999 m³.\n\nBut rule says \"Don't fabricate\" — I need to interpret. The value \"99.999.999\" — if treated as a number with dots as thousands separators, it's 99999999. If the last is decimal, 99999.999.\n\nFor water meters with verification scale interval of 0.0001 m³ minimum... wait, verification scale interval (maximum value) 0,0001 m³ = 0.0001 m³ = 0.1 L. An indicating range minimum value of 99999.999 m³ with VSD of 0.0001 m³? Hmm, that doesn't quite line up—8 digits with 3 decimals gives 99999.999. But VSD of 0.0001 m³ would need 4 decimals.\n\nActually R49-2 requires the indicating range. Let me not overthink. The safest interpretation: \"99.999.999\" is the register full-scale value written with dots as digit grouping: 99 999 999 (99,999,999)? That's odd for m³.\n\nHmm. Actually, in many KROHNE water meter certificates (this is KROHNE Optiflux or WATERFLUX), the indicating device table shows \"Indicating range (minimum value): 99999.999 m³\". I believe the intended value is 99999.999 m³. But given ambiguity, I could keep it as string? The rules say numbers not strings for numeric values. But \"99.999.999\" cannot be directly parsed. Options:\n\n1. Treat dots as thousand separators: 99999999.\n2. Treat last dot as decimal separator: 99999.999.\n3. Keep as string \"99.999.999\".\n\nGiven the decimal comma usage elsewhere in the document (0,0001 → 0.0001), the OCR preserved dots in \"99.999.999\". European convention: dots = thousands separators, comma = decimal. So \"99.999.999\" = 99999999. But a water meter register that counts to 99,999,999 m³... Actually, some registers have 8 digits: 99999.999 m³ requires 3 decimals; 9999999.99 requires 2 decimals; 999999.999...\n\nHmm, let me check KROHNE certificates. R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 is the KROHNE WATERFLUX 3070 / Optiflux? Actually \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" — NMi certificate for KROHNE water meters. The indicating device is an electronic register.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll keep the value as the string \"99.999.999\" — no wait, the rules say numbers not strings. But when the value cannot be reliably parsed... Rule 2 says \"Numbers, not strings.\" For ambiguous thousands formatting, I think the intended reading is 99999.999 m³ — hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Electronic indicating devices on electromagnetic water meters typically display up to 99999.999 m³ (8 characters with 3 decimals) or with more digits. \"99.999.999\" as a minimum indicating range of 99,999,999 doesn't make sense (\"indicating range (minimum value)\" means the minimum full-scale range the register covers — i.e., the register must be able to display at least this value). So the register must display at least 99999.999 m³ or 99,999,999 m³?\n\nFor DN600 water meter with Q3 up to 4000 m³/h, the volume over a year could be large but 100 million m³ display seems excessive. Typical: 99999.999 m³ (100,000 m³) might be small for DN600? 4000 m³/h × 24 h = 96,000 m³/day — so 100,000 m³ would roll over daily! So for DN600, 99999.999 is too small. 99,999,999 (100 million m³) makes more sense for large meters. Hmm, but the table says all meter sizes have the same indicating range minimum value of 99.999.999.\n\nActually with an electronic register, there's no fixed digit count constraint; they specify indicating range as 99,999,999? That seems plausible: the display can show 8 digits without decimals... but then VSD of 0.0001 m³ conflicts.\n\nAlternatively \"99 999.999 m³\" = 99,999.999 m³ ≈ 100,000 m³. Hmm.\n\nLet me just go with parsing it as a string \"99.999.999\" since I can't determine the numeric intent confidently? The instructions say numbers not strings, but also \"Don't fabricate.\" An ambiguous digit-grouping string is best preserved verbatim. However, I think there's a reasonable interpretation: dots as thousands separators → 99999999. The decimal-comma convention in the doc (0,0001) supports dots = thousands separators. So 99.999.999 = 99,999,999 m³.\n\nHmm, but that gives an 8-digit integer register while VSD max is 0.0001 m³ for small meters. VSD = verification scale interval = smallest displayed increment = 0.0001 m³ = 0.1 L. An 8-digit integer display can't show 0.1 L increments. So the register must have decimals. So \"99.999.999\" likely = 99 999.999 (one dot thousands separator, last dot actually decimal but OCR merged, or the register shows \"99999.999\").\n\nActually wait — maybe the format is: the register displays 99999.999 and the cert's \"indicating range (minimum value)\" is 99999.999 m³. In Dutch/European formatting that would be \"99.999,999\" (dot thousands, comma decimal). OCR turned the comma into a dot producing \"99.999.999\". That's consistent with decimal-comma usage in \"0,0001\". So 99999.999 m³.\n\nI'll go with 99999.999. Given the rules: decimal point conversion, numbers not strings. I think 99999.999 is the best interpretation. I'll note this is a judgment call.\n\nVerification scale interval (maximum value):\n- DN25, DN40, DN50: 0,0001 → 0.0001 m³\n- DN65...DN150: 0,001 → 0.001 m³\n- DN200...DN450: 0,01 → 0.01 m³\n- DN500, DN600: 0,1 → 0.1 m³\n\nThis is a config/model matrix by meter size. Since the fragment references \"Meter size\" DN25–DN600, the models are the meter sizes. But this fragment is part 2 — the model family was presumably established in part 1. The instruction says: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\"\n\nSo I should include model_family models? The models are named in the table (DN25 etc.). Since the table varies by meter size, I can either use model_level characteristics with model IDs DN25... or config_level with axis \"meter_size\".\n\nGiven the sizes are groups (DN25, DN40 and DN50 share values), config_level with axis \"meter_size\" seems appropriate. But rule 4 says model family for multiple models; the cert covers many meter sizes. Since part 1 presumably established the models, but in this fragment we see them named. I'll include model_level entries per meter size, or better, config_level with axis \"meter_size\".\n\nHmm. Let me decide: the indicating device characteristics vary by meter size (configuration axis = meter size). I'll use config_level:\n\n```json\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_minimum_value\",\n    \"axis\": \"meter_size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"DN25, DN40, DN50\", \"value\": 99999.999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150\", \"value\": 99999.999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\",\n    \"axis\": \"meter_size\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"DN25, DN40, DN50\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN500, DN600\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nAlso include matrix_tables for the table itself:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"General characteristics of the indicating device\",\n  \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN25, DN40 and DN50\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\": 99999.999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\": 0.0001},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nShould I also add model_family with models DN25...DN600? The rule says include only if cert covers >1 model variant. The cert covers meter sizes DN25–DN600. Since part 1 isn't visible, but the table names them, I could include the models. However, the instruction says extract for content present in this fragment only. The meter sizes are present. I'll include model_family with models list of the 15 sizes? Hmm, but might duplicate part 1 output. The task: \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" I'll include model_family since the fragment shows the cert covers many meter sizes. Actually, to be safe and useful, include models with model_id like \"DN25\", \"DN40\", etc.\n\nHmm, but \"indicating device\" table groups sizes. The models of the certificate are the water meters (e.g., Optiflux / WATERFLUX with DN sizes). I'll list all 15 DN sizes as models.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider — should config-level or model-level? Rule 4 example R137: sizes → model_level entries. So meter sizes → model_level. The values per model:\n\nindicating_range_minimum_value: 99999.999 for all.\nverification_scale_interval_max: varies per size.\n\nmodel_level:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"indicating_range_minimum_value\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\", \"values\": [\n  {\"model\": \"DN25\", \"value\": 99999.999}, {\"model\": \"DN40\", ...}, ...\n]}\n```\n\nThat's 15 entries repeated twice — verbose but consistent with rules. Alternatively config_level with grouped conditions is more compact. Rule 5 says config axes for things like sensor rows, static vs dynamic. Meter size is more like model variants (as in R137 G sizes). I'll use model_level.\n\nHmm, but I'm concerned about fabricating models that part 1 might have described differently. The fragment clearly names DN25–DN600. I'll go with model_level, listing all sizes.\n\nProduction locations: KROHNE Altometer (Dordrecht, NL) and KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). These are manufacturing/production locations. The schema has manufacturers[]. Production locations fit under manufacturers. The applicants/manufacturers were probably in part 1, but this fragment names the production locations. I'll include manufacturers with these two entries.\n\nCertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" with revision 3. Schema: number field. Maybe \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" and revision_history includes revision \"3\". The number includes \"revision 3\" separately in text: \"Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 revision 3\". I'll set number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\".\n- scheme: The number contains \"-A-\" indicating Scheme A. Schema: scheme (A|B). Should I include? The number format R49/2013-A-NL1... The \"A\" indicates scheme A. I can infer \"A\". I'll include scheme: \"A\".\n- project_number: \"3846781\"\n- page_total: 6\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: not stated in fragment (revision 3 date is 30 July 2024; the current issue date might be that). The certificate history table: Initial 26 January 2021; 1: 3 May 2022; 2: 12 June 2023; 3: 30 July 2024. date_issued not explicitly stated — omit or use revision 3 date? Rule 8: don't fabricate. The current revision date is 30 July 2024, which is effectively the issue date of revision 3. I could set date_issued: \"2024-07-30\" based on the history. Hmm, risky but reasonable. I'll omit date_issued to be safe? Actually revision history's latest entry corresponds to the current issue. Many extraction tasks would accept date_issued from the latest revision. I'll include it — actually, let me be conservative and omit it, since the fragment doesn't say \"date of issue\". Hmm.\n\nThe instruction says omit sections not present. Date issued isn't present. I'll omit.\n\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present; omit. (NMi is... its OIML issuer ID might be \"11\" for Netherlands? Not stated. Omit.)\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\" — actually the header says \"NMi\". NMi = Nederlands Meetinstituut. The fragment only shows \"NMi\" logo text. Include name: \"NMi\". Address not present — omit. oiml_issuer_id not present — omit.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\" (canonical from R49/2013...), edition: 2013, scheme: A. accuracy_classes not present. Include recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\"}.\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"Initial\", date: \"2021-01-26\", changes: null or \"-\"}? Schema: revision string e.g. \"0\". The table says \"Initial\". I'll use revision: \"Initial\"... The schema example says revision \"0\" not 0, i.e., string. \"Initial\" is fine as string. changes: \"-\" → maybe omit or null. I'll put changes: null? Rule 8: empty strings not values; use null or omit. \"-\" is a placeholder. I'll set changes: null.\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2022-05-03\", changes: \"Added alternative Modbus PCB components\"}\n- {revision: \"2\", date: \"2023-06-12\", changes: \"Added Alternative Micro Controller Unit (MCU)\"}\n- {revision: \"3\", date: \"2024-07-30\", changes: \"Added second production location\"}\n\ncertified_type: category \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters). The fragment mentions \"The water meter is produced at...\" So category: \"Water meter\". type_designations not present in fragment — omit. module_designation: \"Indicating device\"? Table 3 is about the indicating device, which is a module. Hmm, the table describes \"General characteristics of the indicating device\". Components[] could include the indicating device as a component. I'll add a components entry: role \"indicating device\"? The schema for components: role, type_designations, alternatives, characteristics. No type designation given. I could skip components and just put the characteristics. I think putting the table in matrix_tables + model_level characteristics is enough. Maybe add a component {role: \"indicating device\"} — without type designations it's thin. I'll skip components.\n\ncertified_type.category: \"Water meter\". Include? The fragment says \"water meter\". Yes.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level: none that are whole-cert? The indicating range is same across all models (99999.999). Could be type_level since uniform... but it's presented per-size. I'll keep in model_level for both attributes.\n\nManufacturers/production locations:\n```json\n\"manufacturers\": [\n  {\"name\": \"KROHNE Altometer\", \"address_lines\": [\"Kerkeplaat 12\", \"3313 LC Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]},\n  {\"name\": \"KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd.\", \"address_lines\": [\"No. 555 Minshen Road, Songjiang Industrial Zone\", \"Shanghai 201612\", \"China\"]}\n]\n```\n\nShould I include applicants? Not named in fragment. Omit.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nRegarding the \"99.999.999\" value: I'll interpret as 99999.999. Let me double check plausibility with VSD: R49-2 (2013) Annex... For water meters, the indicating range: \"the indicating device shall have a indicating range of at least ... \" Hmm. Actually I recall NMi certificates for Optiflux 4000/WATERFLUX 3070: \"Table 3 General characteristics of the indicating device\" with indicating range min value 99 999.999 m³ and verification scale interval max values varying by DN. Yes — electronic registers with 8 digits + 3 decimals: 99999.999 m³. And VSD 0.0001 m³ means 4th decimal? Hmm, VSD max 0.0001 m³ — the register could display 99999.9999? Whatever — 99999.999 is my choice. Actually wait, if VSD is 0.0001 m³ = 0.1 L, and register shows 99999.999, the VSD would be 0.001 m³. The \"maximum value\" of VSD is the largest permitted scale interval; actual can be smaller. So register displaying 99999.999 with VSD ≤ 0.0001 would need 99999.9999. Fine — indicating range min value is a requirement, register may show more digits. OK.\n\nSo value: 99999.999, unit_symbol: \"m³\", unit_id: null. (unitsdb id for cubic metre — \"u:cubic_metre\"? Rule says leave null unless certain. I'll leave null.)\n\nDate format: I'll use ISO \"2021-01-26\" etc.\n\nmodel_family: include models list of 15 DN sizes. family_name: null.\n\nActually — should I really enumerate models from this fragment? The meter sizes appear as conditions in the table. R137 example maps table size rows → models. So yes.\n\nModels: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600. That's 16.\n\nWait: DN25, DN40 and DN50 (3); DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125 and DN150 (5); DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400 and DN450 (6); DN500 and DN600 (2). Total 16.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. indicating_range_minimum_value: 16 values all 99999.999.\n2. verification_scale_interval_maximum_value: 16 values (0.0001 ×3, 0.001 ×5, 0.01 ×6, 0.1 ×2).\n\nmatrix_tables: one table with rows as grouped in source. Columns: meter_size, indicating_range_minimum_value_m3, verification_scale_interval_maximum_value_m3. With column_units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"].\n\nAlso certificate page info: \"Page 5 of 6\" and \"Page 6 of 6\" — page_total 6.\n\ncertificate:\n```json\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"3846781\",\n  \"page_total\": 6,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n}\n```\n\nrevision \"3\" noted in number line — the revision_history covers it.\n\nissuing_authority: {\"name\": \"NMi\"} — maybe full name \"NMi (Nederlands Meetinstituut)\"? Only \"NMi\" shown. Use \"NMi\".\n\ncertified_type: {\"category\": \"Water meter\", \"module_designation\": \"Indicating device\"}? module_designation per schema examples: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. The cert is for water meters (complete instruments?) with an indicating device. Hmm, R49-2 water meters — indicating device is part. I'll set module_designation: \"Indicating device\"? Actually the table is about the indicating device characteristics of the certified type. I could add components: [{role: \"indicating device\", type_designations: [], characteristics...}] — characteristics nested? The schema for components has characteristics{} (free object). I'll add a component entry with role \"indicating device\" and characteristics with the two attributes? But I already put them in characteristics.model_level. Duplication risk. I'll skip components.\n\nOutput JSON:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3846781\",\n    \"page_total\": 6,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"KROHNE Altometer\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Kerkeplaat 12\", \"3313 LC Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"No. 555 Minshen Road, Songjiang Industrial Zone\", \"Shanghai 201612\", \"China\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN25\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN50\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN65\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN80\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN100\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN125\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN150\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN200\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN250\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN300\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN350\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN400\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN450\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN500\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN600\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"indicating device\",\n      \"type_designations\": [],\n      ...\n    }\n  ],\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nSkip components.\n\ncharacteristics:\n```json\n{\n  \"model_level\": [\n    {\n      \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_minimum_value\",\n      \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\",\n      \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"DN25\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN50\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN65\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN80\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN100\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN125\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN150\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN200\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN250\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN300\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN350\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN400\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN450\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN500\", \"value\": 99999.999},\n        {\"model\": \"DN600\", \"value\": 99999.999}\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\",\n      \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\",\n      \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"DN25\", \"value\": 0.0001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 0.0001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN50\", \"value\": 0.0001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN65\", \"value\": 0.001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN80\", \"value\": 0.001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN100\", \"value\": 0.001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN125\", \"value\": 0.001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN150\", \"value\": 0.001},\n        {\"model\": \"DN200\", \"value\": 0.01},\n        {\"model\": \"DN250\", \"value\": 0.01},\n        {\"model\": \"DN300\", \"value\": 0.01},\n        {\"model\": \"DN350\", \"value\": 0.01},\n        {\"model\": \"DN400\", \"value\": 0.01},\n        {\"model\": \"DN450\", \"value\": 0.01},\n        {\"model\": \"DN500\", \"value\": 0.1},\n        {\"model\": \"DN600\", \"value\": 0.1}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nmatrix_tables:\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"General characteristics of the indicating device\",\n    \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\"],\n    \"column_units\": [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"],\n    \"rows\": [\n      {\"meter_size\": \"DN25, DN40, DN50\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\": 99999.999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\": 0.0001},\n      {\"meter_size\": \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\": 99999.999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\": 0.001},\n      {\"meter_size\": \"DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\": 99999.999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\": 0.01},\n      {\"meter_size\": \"DN500, DN600\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\": 99999.999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_value\": 0.1}\n    ],\n    \"description\": \"Minimum indicating range and maximum verification scale interval of the indicating device by meter size\",\n    \"footnotes\": []\n  }\n]\n```\n\nrecommendation:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"R49\",\n  \"edition\": 2013,\n  \"scheme\": \"A\"\n}\n```\n\nrevision_history:\n```json\n[\n  {\"revision\": \"Initial\", \"date\": \"2021-01-26\", \"changes\": null},\n  {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2022-05-03\", \"changes\": \"Added alternative Modbus PCB components\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"2\", \"date\": \"2023-06-12\", \"changes\": \"Added Alternative Micro Controller Unit (MCU)\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"3\", \"date\": \"2024-07-30\", \"changes\": \"Added second production location\"}\n]\n```\n\nWait — should revision \"Initial\" be \"0\"? The schema says revision string e.g. \"0\" not 0. Table says \"Initial\". I'll use \"Initial\" verbatim. Fine.\n\nShould I include date_issued? Not stated; omit.\n\nOne more consideration: \"99.999.999\" — should I add a note? No commentary allowed. I'll go with 99999.999.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider once more. If dots were thousands separators (consistent with decimal-comma in doc), value = 99,999,999 m³ = 9.9999999e7. For a DN600 water meter at, say, 4000 m³/h running continuously: 4000×8760 ≈ 35 million m³/year. 100 million m³ ≈ 3 years. Hmm plausible too. For DN25, 3.5 m³/h × 8760 = 30,660 m³/year — 100 million = 3260 years. The register is 8 digits: \"99999999\" integer vs \"99999.999\".\n\nCommon water meter electronic registers (e.g., KROHNE) display 8 digits with fraction. The \"indicating range (minimum value)\" in NMi certs I've seen: \"99999.999 m³\". Let me think about the source: This is certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 for KROHNE... The original PDF likely shows \"99 999.999 m³\" with a space thousands separator and decimal point (since English version of Dutch cert uses comma as thousands? Or point?). English documents typically use comma thousands + dot decimal: \"99,999.999\". OCR output \"99.999.999\" — dots replacing commas and the decimal point. Most likely original: \"99,999.999\" → 99999.999. I'm fairly confident. Go with 99999.999.\n\nAlso consider including \"indicating_range\" values as type_level since identical for all models? Keep model_level for consistency.\n\nShould the config for indicating device go under config_level with axis \"meter_size\"? I chose model_level per R137 precedent (size → models). Good.\n\nFinal JSON — ensure valid JSON, no trailing commas, no markdown fences.\n\nAlso, should certificate include \"revision\": \"3\"? Schema has no revision field at certificate level; revision_history covers it. The number line includes \"revision 3\". I'll leave it out of number, keep number clean.\n\nOne more: \"Page 5 of 6\" — page_total 6. Good.",
